+## --enable-libgpiod <a id="libgpiod"></a>
+
+By default, the front panel drivers use the vendored [pinctrl][] library,
+which mmaps the GPIO peripheral registers through `/dev/gpiomem`.  That
+device only exists with the Raspberry Pi foundation's out-of-tree
+`gpiomem` driver, so it is present on Raspberry Pi OS but absent from
+distro kernels — Fedora among them.
+
+With `--enable-libgpiod`, the same panel logic links instead against a
+second backend (`src/pinctrl/gpiolib-gpiod.c`) that talks to the kernel
+GPIO character device through libgpiod (2.0 or newer).  Use it when
+building a package for a distro kernel, or any time you'd rather not
+depend on the out-of-tree driver.
+
+Requirements:
+
+*   `libgpiod-devel` (or the `pkgconfig(libgpiod)` equivalent) at build
+    time.
+*   The user running `pidp8i` needs read/write access to `/dev/gpiochip0`;
+    the RPM grants this via 99-pidp8i.rules (group `pidp8i`), not `gpio`.
+*   No other driver may hold the pins the panel uses, in particular
+    GPIOs 14/15 on Pi 3-family boards, which are also the Bluetooth
+    UART lines: `dtoverlay=disable-bt` and a disabled serial console
+    free them up.
+
+[pinctrl]: https://github.com/raspberrypi/utils/tree/master/pinctrl
+
+
 ## --throttle <a id="throttle"></a>
 
 See [`README-throttle.md`][thro] for the values this option takes.  If
